A pen by my nose—  
ink heats on paper cold;  
page opens its mouth.

In ink I carve out my sentence’s thick lip—  
then smear it clean with water to rinse off dust;  
each word is a stone in the gutter—my eye  
reads each one, then turns back and smashes them.

I turn pages until my hand hurts from turning;  
the room holds its breath while I rewrite hard;  
pages fold over their own mistakes like paper—  
I rip them up, lay out new lines on fresh ink.

And finally, the blank page stands tall—my brain  
a clean slate where ideas spill out in rows;  
then, one by one, they climb onto a line and stand:  
the argument I’ve been waiting for all night.

Switching from one task to another like  
a gear in a machine grinding gears inside my skull—  
notes left half-written; phone buzzing;  
my hand pauses over the keyboard, then flies back—

words spill out, clean at first, then muddy;  
the light flickers on a desk lamp’s bulb, casting  
tiny shadows that dart across blank screens;  
and somewhere, in the corner of my vision,  
a doorbell rings, or someone coughs—  
all these small urgencies tugging at me like strings.
